<section id="research" class="paper-section">
  <div class="container paper-shell">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-kicker">Research</span>
      <h2>Formalizing federated PKI as a governed weighted graph.</h2>
      <p>
        The proposal introduces a formal layer above PKIX semantics, representing
        certification authorities and governed federation relationships as a directed,
        weighted graph that can be queried for valid and optimal certification paths.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="research-grid">
      <article class="research-card">
        <div class="research-card-header">
          <h3>Governed trust graph</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          Instead of inheriting trust from entire hierarchies, PolarisChain models
          trust as explicit graph edges between governed certification authorities.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Vertices represent trust anchors, bridge CAs, issuing CAs, and other governed trust points.</li>
          <li>Edges encode admissible inter-domain relationships instead of broad hierarchy inheritance.</li>
          <li>The model preserves PKIX validation while making federation semantics explicit and queryable.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="research-card research-card-figure">
        <div class="mini-chart" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="mini-chart-toolbar">
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
          </div>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" class="mini-chart-svg">
            <defs>
              <linearGradient id="chartArea" x1="0" x2="0" y1="0" y2="1">
                <stop offset="0%" stop-color="rgba(90, 122, 166, 0.24)" />
                <stop offset="100%" stop-color="rgba(90, 122, 166, 0)" />
              </linearGradient>
            </defs>
            <path d="M18 150 L18 18 L302 18" class="mini-chart-axis"></path>
            <path d="M18 150 C60 145, 85 132, 110 98 S170 42, 204 62 S266 86, 302 54" class="mini-chart-line"></path>
            <path d="M18 150 C60 145, 85 132, 110 98 S170 42, 204 62 S266 86, 302 54 L302 150 Z" class="mini-chart-area"></path>
            <circle cx="110" cy="98" r="4" class="mini-chart-point"></circle>
            <circle cx="204" cy="62" r="4" class="mini-chart-point"></circle>
            <circle cx="302" cy="54" r="4" class="mini-chart-point"></circle>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="figure-caption">
          <h3>Topology growth observation</h3>
          <p>
            As the number of federation links grows, candidate certification paths
            grow rapidly, making uncontrolled exploration harder to govern and justify.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="research-card">
        <div class="research-card-header">
          <h3>Optimal path semantics</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          The proposal defines a multicriteria cost over federation edges so path
          selection becomes an optimization problem rather than a heuristic search.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Edge weights capture cryptographic profile, algorithm family, key strength, and structural distance.</li>
          <li>Dijkstra-based traversal computes a deterministic minimum-cost route over admissible paths.</li>
          <li>The result is an explainable certification chain between issuer-side entities and governed trust points.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="model-section" aria-labelledby="mathematical-model-heading">
      <div class="model-heading">
        <span class="note-label">Mathematical model</span>
        <h3 id="mathematical-model-heading">A compact formalization of federation and cost.</h3>
        <p>
          The graph abstracts federated PKI into a structure that can be validated,
          scored, and optimized without changing underlying certificate semantics.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="model-grid">
        <article class="research-card model-card model-card-formula">
          <span class="model-card-label">Topological model</span>
          <div class="equation-stack equation-stack-paper" aria-label="Graph equations">
            <p class="equation-intro">
              Federated PKI is formalized as a directed weighted graph, where each
              admissible trust relation receives a multicriteria cost:
            </p>

            <div class="paper-equation" aria-label="G equals open parenthesis V comma E comma w close parenthesis, where w maps E to non-negative real numbers, and w of e equals f of crypto of e, alg of e, key of e, and dist of e">
              <span class="paper-equation-term">
                <span class="math-var">G</span> = (<span class="math-var">V</span>, <span class="math-var">E</span>, <span class="math-var">w</span>)
              </span>
              <span class="paper-equation-word">where</span>
              <span class="paper-equation-term">
                <span class="math-var">w</span> : <span class="math-var">E</span> &rarr; ℝ<sub>&ge;0</sub>,
              </span>
              <span class="paper-equation-term">
                <span class="math-var">w</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>) = <span class="math-var">f</span>(<span class="math-var">crypto</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">alg</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">key</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">dist</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>))
              </span>
              <span class="paper-equation-number">(1)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Vertices represent governed trust entities, edges represent admissible
            trust relationships, and the weight function assigns a comparable cost to
            traversing each relationship.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="research-card model-card">
          <h3>Weight components</h3>
          <dl class="model-terms">
            <div>
              <dt>crypto(e)</dt>
              <dd>Classical, hybrid, or post-quantum cryptographic profile assigned to the edge.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>alg(e)</dt>
              <dd>Algorithm family used by the relationship, such as RSA, ECDSA, or ML-DSA.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>key(e)</dt>
              <dd>Security strength associated with the keys and resulting assurance level.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>dist(e)</dt>
              <dd>Structural complexity introduced by traversing between PKI domains or governance zones.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="research-notes">
      <div>
        <span class="note-label">Research problem</span>
        <p>
          Certification path discovery becomes increasingly difficult as multidomain PKI federations grow denser.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="note-label">Methodological lens</span>
        <p>
          Directed weighted graphs, deterministic shortest-path computation, and policy-aware trust modelling.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="interoperability">
        <span class="note-label">Interoperability objective</span>
        <p>
          Support controlled interoperability across classical, hybrid, and post-quantum PKI domains.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

